{"title": "Fractions: Equivalent Fractions and Comparing Unlike Denominators", "intro": "Tonight we will look at two important skills: understanding when fractions are equal (equivalent fractions), and learning how to compare fractions that have different bottom numbers (unlike denominators).", "sections": [{"heading": "Equivalent Fractions: Same Amount, Different Pictures", "points": ["An equivalent fraction is a fraction that shows the same amount as another, even though the numerator and denominator look different.", "When you make an equivalent fraction, you must change BOTH the numerator and the denominator in the same way.", "If you multiply the denominator by 2, you must also multiply the numerator by 2.", "Using bar models helps: if you split each shaded part into 2 smaller parts, you have twice as many shaded parts and twice as many total parts."], "example": "1/2 is equivalent to 2/4. The bar for 1/2 has 1 shaded part out of 2 total parts. If you split each part in half, you now have 2 shaded parts out of 4 total parts. Same amount of the bar is coloured, but now we say 2/4 instead of 1/2."}, {"heading": "Comparing Fractions with Unlike Denominators", "points": ["When fractions have different denominators, you cannot just look at the denominator to decide which is bigger.", "Draw both fractions as bar models using bars of the same total length so you can see which one is shaded more.", "The fraction with more of its bar shaded is the bigger fraction, no matter what the numbers say.", "You can arrange fractions from smallest to biggest (ascending order) or biggest to smallest (descending order) by comparing their bar models."], "example": "Compare 1/3 and 1/4. Draw two bars of the same length. Split one into 3 equal parts and shade 1 part. Split the other into 4 equal parts and shade 1 part. You can see 1/3 has more shaded area than 1/4, so 1/3 is bigger. The bigger denominator (4) gave us smaller pieces."}, {"heading": "A Helpful Trick: Compare Fractions with the Same Top Number First", "points": ["When the numerator is the same, the fraction with the smaller denominator is always bigger.", "This is because a bigger denominator means the whole is cut into more pieces, so each piece is smaller.", "For example: 1/2 is bigger than 1/8 because in 1/2, each piece is bigger than in 1/8."], "example": "Look at 1/2, 1/5, and 1/10. All have numerator 1. The bar for 1/2 shows the largest shaded piece, 1/5 shows a smaller piece, and 1/10 shows the tiniest piece. So: 1/2 > 1/5 > 1/10."}], "remember": ["Always change BOTH numbers (numerator and denominator) by the same amount to make an equivalent fraction.", "To compare fractions, draw bar models that are the same length so you can see which has more shading.", "A bigger denominator does not make a fraction bigger—it usually makes each piece smaller.", "Check both the top number (numerator) and the bottom number (denominator) together when comparing or making equivalent fractions.", "Use bar models whenever you are not sure—they show you the real answer."]}
